!SSHd - an SSH server for RISC OS 5
====================================

Double-click !SSHd: it puts an icon on the icon bar and listens on TCP
port 22, accepting public-key logins only.

Icon bar
--------
  Select  opens the log window (follows new lines, like tail -f)
  Adjust  opens the Keys window
  Menu    Log window / Keys... / Quit

Keys window
-----------
Lists the public keys that may log in, each with a Delete button, and
offers three ways to add one:
  - type or paste it into the field (the format ssh-keygen writes to
    id_ed25519.pub or id_rsa.pub), then Add or Return;
  - drop such a file onto the window;
  - Fetch: enter the account (user@host) and password of the machine
    whose keys you want to allow.  SSHd logs in there over SSH, shows the
    machine's host key fingerprint for you to confirm, reads
    ~/.ssh/id_*.pub and imports them.  The password is used once and not
    stored.  The desktop pauses while this runs.
Keys are stored in <Choices$Write>.SSHd.authkeys, one per line.
The window opens by itself when no key is configured yet.  The server's
own host key is shown at the bottom; it is generated on first run with the
CryptRandom module and kept in Choices:SSHd.hostkey.

Use
---
  ssh user@riscos-host '*Cat'      run one command, output comes back
  ssh user@riscos-host             interactive * command line in a
                                   TaskWindow; Ctrl-D ends the session

The user name is not checked; only the key is.  Single commands run through
OS_CLI inside the server's own task with their VDU output captured to a
file, so they cannot be interactive.  An interactive session is a real
TaskWindow child task (as used by Edit), so it behaves like a task window:
line input, Escape via Ctrl-C, and the same limits for Wimp applications.

Options (edit !Run, or run sshd by hand in a TaskWindow with -t):
  -p port    listen on another port
  -d dir     use another configuration directory
  -t         text mode: no icon bar, log to the TaskWindow, Escape quits
  -v         verbose logging (-vv includes SSH protocol messages)
